\begin{abstract}
Driving simulation and scene editing require completing unseen surfaces exposed by camera or actor motion, while ensuring that completed actor surfaces follow vehicle motion and human articulation. We present DriveCompose, a framework for generative reconstruction of compositional driving scenes from image sequences. We represent the background in world coordinates and vehicles and humans in their respective canonical frames, allowing completed surfaces to follow vehicle motion and human articulation. A conditional flow-matching model jointly completes missing background and actor content in these representations, using cross-component memory to share scene context. Completion is constrained by the input observations to preserve the geometry and appearance of observed surfaces. We render the completed scene at the recorded camera viewpoints and actor states to check its consistency with the input images. On Waymo, DriveCompose improves dynamic scene reconstruction and off-trajectory view synthesis over the evaluated baselines. Compared with the variant without cross-component memory, our model reduces mean absolute RGB error on unseen surfaces by 20.5\% in component ablations. The resulting Gaussian scene supports composition and rendering with the same completed assets under changes in camera viewpoint, vehicle pose, and human articulation.
\end{abstract}

